IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pha-offences`

-- Protection from Harassment Act 2014, Part 2 -- worked cases.
--
-- Where the Act supplies an Illustration, the case is taken from it and the
-- Illustration is named. Everything else is a claim about the ENCODING on stated
-- facts, not about the law.

§ `Fixtures`

GIVEN `threatening` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `abusive` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `insulting` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `sexual` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `indecent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Quality
DECIDE `quality` `threatening` `abusive` `insulting` `sexual` `indecent` IS
    Quality WITH
        `threatening` IS `threatening`
        `abusive` IS `abusive`
        `insulting` IS `insulting`
        `sexual` IS `sexual`
        `indecent` IS `indecent`

`threatening only` MEANS `quality` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`abusive and insulting` MEANS `quality` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
`insulting and sexual` MEANS `quality` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
`sexual only` MEANS `quality` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`indecent only` MEANS `quality` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
`abusive and indecent` MEANS `quality` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE
`none of the five` MEANS `quality` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

§ `Section 2 -- the five qualities, and the two lists`

#ASSERT `the words or communication are of a quality sections 3, 4 and 6 name` `threatening only`
#ASSERT `the words or communication are of a quality sections 3, 4 and 6 name` `sexual only`
#ASSERT `the words or communication are of a quality sections 3, 4 and 6 name` `indecent only`
#ASSERT NOT `the words or communication are of a quality sections 3, 4 and 6 name` `none of the five`

-- s 5(1) names only the first three. Indecency alone cannot reach it.
#ASSERT `the words or communication are of a quality section 5(1) names` `threatening only`
#ASSERT `the words or communication are of a quality section 5(1) names` `abusive and insulting`
#ASSERT NOT `the words or communication are of a quality section 5(1) names` `sexual only`
#ASSERT NOT `the words or communication are of a quality section 5(1) names` `indecent only`

§ `Section 3 -- intent, conduct, and a result that may land on anyone`

GIVEN `intent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the quality` IS A Quality,
      `words` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `identity information` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `target harmed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `other harmed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Section3Facts
DECIDE `section 3 facts` `intent` `the quality` `words` `identity information` `target harmed` `other harmed` `reasonable` IS
    Section3Facts WITH
        `intent to cause harassment, alarm, distress or humiliation to the target person` IS `intent`
        `the quality of the words, behaviour or communication` IS `the quality`
        `used words or behaviour, or made a communication` IS `words`
        `published identity information of the target person or a related person` IS `identity information`
        `as a result the target person was caused harassment, alarm, distress or humiliation` IS `target harmed`
        `as a result some other person was caused harassment, alarm, distress or humiliation` IS `other harmed`
        `the conduct was reasonable` IS `reasonable`

-- Illustration (a) to s 3: X describes a desire for a sexual relationship with
-- the coworker Y in an insulting manner, knows Y is within earshot, intends
-- distress, and Y is distressed.
`Illustration 3(a) -- the coworker within earshot` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` TRUE `insulting and sexual` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration (b): X writes a threatening letter intending to alarm Y, throws
-- it away, Y finds it and is alarmed. The Act says X is not guilty because X
-- "had no reason to believe that the letter would be seen by Y". There is no
-- express element in s 3(1) that carries that reasoning, and s 3(3) gives only
-- a reasonableness defence -- unlike s 4(3)(a), which has a no-reason-to-believe
-- defence. The encoding records the Illustration's facts without being able to
-- produce its answer; see NOTES.md.
`Illustration 3(b) -- the discarded letter` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` TRUE `threatening only` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration (c): X posts abusive remarks about Y, then posts Y's photographs
-- and mobile number intending to cause harassment by facilitating contact. Y
-- never sees the posts, and is harassed by strangers who did. This is the
-- bystander-free case: Y is the target and Y is harmed, just not by seeing it.
`Illustration 3(c) -- the doxxing post` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` TRUE `abusive and insulting` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- The variant that matters for s 12(9): the target never learns of it, and a
-- family member is the one distressed.
`a doxxing post that distressed only a relative` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` TRUE `abusive and insulting` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- Illustration (d): X posts a video of Y driving recklessly, intending to warn
-- others to drive defensively. No intent to harass.
`Illustration 3(d) -- the dashcam warning` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` FALSE `none of the five` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- Intent and conduct but no result at all.
`intent and abuse but nobody was harmed` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` TRUE `abusive and insulting` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Result and conduct but no intent -- which is s 4's territory, not s 3's.
`abuse that harmed, with no intent` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` FALSE `abusive and insulting` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- Everything made out, and the conduct was reasonable.
`a contravention where the conduct was reasonable` MEANS
    `section 3 facts` TRUE `abusive and insulting` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE

#ASSERT `section 3(1) is contravened` `Illustration 3(a) -- the coworker within earshot`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 3(2) is committed` `Illustration 3(a) -- the coworker within earshot`

-- Illustration (c): the identity-information limb, and a target harmed without
-- ever seeing the post.
#ASSERT `a limb of section 3(1) conduct is made out` `Illustration 3(c) -- the doxxing post`
#ASSERT `section 3(1) is contravened` `Illustration 3(c) -- the doxxing post`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 3(2) is committed` `Illustration 3(c) -- the doxxing post`

-- The result may fall on someone other than the target. s 3(1) names "the
-- target person or any other person" as the victim.
#ASSERT `section 3(1) is contravened` `a doxxing post that distressed only a relative`

-- All three elements are needed.
#ASSERT NOT `section 3(1) is contravened` `Illustration 3(d) -- the dashcam warning`
#ASSERT NOT `section 3(1) is contravened` `intent and abuse but nobody was harmed`
#ASSERT NOT `section 3(1) is contravened` `abuse that harmed, with no intent`

-- s 3(3): the defence answers the offence and leaves the contravention standing.
-- The pair is the point; see pha-civil-cases.l4.
#ASSERT `section 3(1) is contravened` `a contravention where the conduct was reasonable`
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 3(2) is committed` `a contravention where the conduct was reasonable`

-- The Illustration the encoding cannot reproduce. s 3(1) has no
-- no-reason-to-believe element and s 3(3) has no such defence, so on the stated
-- facts the encoding finds a contravention where the Act says there is no
-- offence. Asserted deliberately, as a record that the encoding and the
-- Illustration disagree.
#ASSERT `section 3(1) is contravened` `Illustration 3(b) -- the discarded letter`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 3(2) is committed` `Illustration 3(b) -- the discarded letter`

§ `Section 4 -- no intent, no result, and no identity-information limb`

GIVEN `the quality` IS A Quality,
      `words` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `identity information` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `perceived by someone likely to be harmed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `legitimate purpose` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `no reason to believe` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Section4Facts
DECIDE `section 4 facts` `the quality` `words` `identity information` `perceived by someone likely to be harmed` `legitimate purpose` `no reason to believe` `reasonable` IS
    Section4Facts WITH
        `the quality of the words, behaviour or communication` IS `the quality`
        `used words or behaviour, or made a communication` IS `words`
        `published identity information of the victim or a related person` IS `identity information`
        `heard, seen or otherwise perceived by a person likely to be caused harassment, alarm, distress or humiliation` IS `perceived by someone likely to be harmed`
        `has a legitimate purpose related to science, medicine, education or art` IS `legitimate purpose`
        `no reason to believe it would be perceived by the victim` IS `no reason to believe`
        `the conduct was reasonable` IS `reasonable`

-- Illustration (a) to s 4: X posts a vulgar tirade against the classmate Y on a
-- website their classmates can see; a classmate shows it to Y, who is
-- distressed.
`Illustration 4(a) -- the vulgar tirade` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `abusive and insulting` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration (c): X brandishes a sword in a bus station and several people are
-- alarmed. "X is guilty of an offence under this section even though X's actions
-- were not directed at anyone." Behaviour, not words.
`Illustration 4(c) -- the sword in the bus station` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `threatening only` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration (d): a counselling supervisor circulates training material with
-- case studies of sexually harassing posts in an internal messaging group. A
-- legitimate purpose related to education.
`Illustration 4(d) -- the counsellors' training material` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `sexual only` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration (e): X posts sex-education graphics in a work messaging group,
-- unrelated to the group's work, and continues despite requests to stop. Not a
-- legitimate purpose.
`Illustration 4(e) -- the unwanted sex-education graphics` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `indecent only` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- The variant that shows how far s 4(1A) reaches: the same material, but the
-- communication is also abusive.
`educational material that is also abusive` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `abusive and indecent` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- The doxxer who is merely careless. A home address is not threatening, abusive,
-- insulting, sexual or indecent, and s 4 has no limb for identity information.
`a published home address, carelessly` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `none of the five` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE

`abuse nobody could have been expected to see` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `abusive and insulting` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE

`abuse that was reasonable in the circumstances` MEANS
    `section 4 facts` `abusive and insulting` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE

#ASSERT `section 4(1) is contravened` `Illustration 4(a) -- the vulgar tirade`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 4(2) is committed` `Illustration 4(a) -- the vulgar tirade`

-- s 4 needs no intent and no actual harm -- only that a person who perceived it
-- was likely to be caused the harm. Illustration (c) says so expressly: X's
-- actions were not directed at anyone.
#ASSERT `section 4(1) is contravened` `Illustration 4(c) -- the sword in the bus station`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 4(2) is committed` `Illustration 4(c) -- the sword in the bus station`

-- s 4(1A) is a non-contravention and it reaches the sexual and indecent limbs.
#ASSERT `section 4(1A) takes the conduct outside subsection (1)` `Illustration 4(d) -- the counsellors' training material`
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(1) is contravened` `Illustration 4(d) -- the counsellors' training material`
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(1A) takes the conduct outside subsection (1)` `Illustration 4(e) -- the unwanted sex-education graphics`
#ASSERT `section 4(1) is contravened` `Illustration 4(e) -- the unwanted sex-education graphics`

-- And it reaches only those limbs. Material with a genuine educational purpose
-- that is also abusive is outside s 4(1A) and must fall back on s 4(3)(b).
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(1A) takes the conduct outside subsection (1)` `educational material that is also abusive`
#ASSERT `section 4(1) is contravened` `educational material that is also abusive`

-- THE DOXXING GAP. s 3 has an identity-information limb and needs intent; s 4
-- needs no intent and has no identity-information limb. So publishing a home
-- address to a hostile audience, where intent cannot be proved, contravenes
-- neither.
#ASSERT NOT `section 4(1) is contravened` `a published home address, carelessly`
#ASSERT NOT `section 3(1) is contravened` (`section 3 facts` FALSE `none of the five` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE)

-- The two s 4(3) defences answer the offence and leave the contravention.
#ASSERT `section 4(1) is contravened` `abuse nobody could have been expected to see`
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 4(2) is committed` `abuse nobody could have been expected to see`
#ASSERT `section 4(1) is contravened` `abuse that was reasonable in the circumstances`
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 4(2) is committed` `abuse that was reasonable in the circumstances`

§ `Section 5 -- violence, and the defence that is confined to one limb`

GIVEN `the quality` IS A Quality,
      `words` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `identity information` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `intent to cause belief` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `intent to provoke` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `intent to facilitate` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `likely belief` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `likely provocation` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `knew it likely` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `no reason to believe` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Section5Facts
DECIDE `section 5 facts` `the quality` `words` `identity information` `intent to cause belief` `intent to provoke` `intent to facilitate` `likely belief` `likely provocation` `knew it likely` `no reason to believe` `reasonable` IS
    Section5Facts WITH
        `the quality of the words, behaviour or communication` IS `the quality`
        `used words or behaviour towards another person, or made a communication to them` IS `words`
        `published identity information of the victim or a related person` IS `identity information`
        `intent to cause the victim to believe unlawful violence will be used` IS `intent to cause belief`
        `intent to provoke the use of unlawful violence` IS `intent to provoke`
        `intent to facilitate the use of unlawful violence` IS `intent to facilitate`
        `the victim is likely to believe such violence will be used` IS `likely belief`
        `it is likely that such violence will be provoked` IS `likely provocation`
        `knew or had reasonable cause to believe it likely to cause such belief or facilitate such violence` IS `knew it likely`
        `no reason to believe it would be perceived by the victim` IS `no reason to believe`
        `the conduct was reasonable` IS `reasonable`

-- Illustration (a) to s 5: X posts threatening and abusive remarks about the
-- classmate Y, then posts Y's identity information with "Everyone, let's beat Y
-- up!". The Act says X is guilty in respect of the subsequent post -- the
-- s 5(1A) limb.
`Illustration 5(a) -- lets beat Y up` MEANS
    `section 5 facts` `threatening only` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration (c): X posts threats against Y on a platform Y cannot see, calling
-- on others to hunt Y down; B replies with Y's home address. "B is guilty of an
-- offence under this section." B published identity information knowing it
-- likely to facilitate violence.
`Illustration 5(c) -- B posts the address` MEANS
    `section 5 facts` `none of the five` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- s 5(1)(b) without intent: the victim is likely to believe violence will be
-- used, and the accused had no reason to think the victim would see it.
`threats the victim was never meant to see` MEANS
    `section 5 facts` `threatening only` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- The same, but the accused intended the victim to believe it. s 5(3)(a) is
-- confined to a contravention of subsection (1)(b), so it is not available.
`threats intended to frighten, sent where the victim might not see them` MEANS
    `section 5 facts` `threatening only` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- Identity information published with knowledge it was likely to facilitate
-- violence, and the accused says they did not expect the victim to see the post.
-- s 5(3)(a) does not reach s 5(1A) either.
`an address posted to a mob, the victim not expected to see it` MEANS
    `section 5 facts` `none of the five` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE

-- Indecency alone, with a likely belief in violence. s 5(1) does not list the
-- indecent limb.
`indecent words and a fear of violence` MEANS
    `section 5 facts` `indecent only` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 5(1A) is made out` `Illustration 5(a) -- lets beat Y up`
#ASSERT `section 5 is contravened` `Illustration 5(a) -- lets beat Y up`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 5(2) is committed` `Illustration 5(a) -- lets beat Y up`

#ASSERT `section 5(1A) is made out` `Illustration 5(c) -- B posts the address`
#ASSERT `section 5 is contravened` `Illustration 5(c) -- B posts the address`

-- s 5(1A)(b) is satisfied by knowledge or reasonable cause to believe -- lower
-- than the intent s 5(1A)(a) requires, and higher than the bare likelihood in
-- s 5(1)(b).
#ASSERT NOT `Illustration 5(c) -- B posts the address`'s `intent to facilitate the use of unlawful violence`
#ASSERT `Illustration 5(c) -- B posts the address`'s `knew or had reasonable cause to believe it likely to cause such belief or facilitate such violence`

-- The s 5(3)(a) defence, and the two places it does not reach.
#ASSERT `section 5(1)(b) is made out` `threats the victim was never meant to see`
#ASSERT `the section 5(3)(a) defence is available` `threats the victim was never meant to see`
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 5(2) is committed` `threats the victim was never meant to see`

#ASSERT `section 5(1)(a) is made out` `threats intended to frighten, sent where the victim might not see them`
#ASSERT NOT `the section 5(3)(a) defence is available` `threats intended to frighten, sent where the victim might not see them`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 5(2) is committed` `threats intended to frighten, sent where the victim might not see them`

#ASSERT `section 5(1A) is made out` `an address posted to a mob, the victim not expected to see it`
#ASSERT NOT `the section 5(3)(a) defence is available` `an address posted to a mob, the victim not expected to see it`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 5(2) is committed` `an address posted to a mob, the victim not expected to see it`

-- Indecency alone cannot reach s 5(1), however frightening the result.
#ASSERT NOT `section 5(1)(b) is made out` `indecent words and a fear of violence`
#ASSERT NOT `section 5 is contravened` `indecent words and a fear of violence`

§ `Section 6 -- the condition that is not a defence`

GIVEN `the quality` IS A Quality,
      `words` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `public servant` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `in relation to duty` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `knew the capacity` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `no reason to believe` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Section6Facts
DECIDE `section 6 facts` `the quality` `words` `public servant` `in relation to duty` `knew the capacity` `no reason to believe` `reasonable` IS
    Section6Facts WITH
        `the quality of the words, behaviour or communication` IS `the quality`
        `used words or behaviour towards the victim, or made a communication to them` IS `words`
        `published identity information of the victim or a related person` IS FALSE
        `the victim is a public servant or public service worker` IS `public servant`
        `the conduct was in relation to the execution of the victim's duty` IS `in relation to duty`
        `knew or ought reasonably to have known the victim was acting in that capacity` IS `knew the capacity`
        `no reason to believe it would be perceived by the victim` IS `no reason to believe`
        `the conduct was reasonable` IS `reasonable`

`abuse of a bus driver at the wheel` MEANS
    `section 6 facts` `abusive and insulting` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE

-- s 6(2): no offence unless the accused knew or ought reasonably to have known
-- the victim was acting in that capacity. A plain-clothes officer off duty in
-- appearance.
`abuse of someone not known to be on duty` MEANS
    `section 6 facts` `abusive and insulting` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE

`abuse of a public servant about something unrelated to their work` MEANS
    `section 6 facts` `abusive and insulting` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE

#ASSERT `section 6(1) conduct is made out` `abuse of a bus driver at the wheel`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 6 is committed` `abuse of a bus driver at the wheel`

-- The s 6(2) condition. The conduct limb is made out and the offence is not,
-- because s 6(2) says no offence is committed unless the accused knew.
#ASSERT `section 6(1) conduct is made out` `abuse of someone not known to be on duty`
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 6 is committed` `abuse of someone not known to be on duty`

#ASSERT NOT `section 6(1) conduct is made out` `abuse of a public servant about something unrelated to their work`
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 6 is committed` `abuse of a public servant about something unrelated to their work`

§ `Section 7 -- the course of conduct`

GIVEN `occasions` IS A NUMBER,
      `protracted` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `previous conviction` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `associated acts` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `caused harm` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `intended` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `ought to have known` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `under written law` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `crime prevention` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `national security` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A StalkingFacts
DECIDE `stalking facts` `occasions` `protracted` `previous conviction` `associated acts` `caused harm` `intended` `ought to have known` `reasonable` `under written law` `crime prevention` `national security` `certificate` IS
    StalkingFacts WITH
        `the number of occasions` IS `occasions`
        `the conduct is protracted` IS `protracted`
        `a previous conviction under section 7 in respect of the same victim` IS `previous conviction`
        `involves acts or omissions associated with stalking` IS `associated acts`
        `causes harassment, alarm, distress or humiliation to the victim` IS `caused harm`
        `intends to cause harassment, alarm, distress or humiliation` IS `intended`
        `a reasonable person with the same information would think it likely to have that effect` IS `ought to have known`
        `the course of conduct was reasonable in all the circumstances` IS `reasonable`
        `pursued under any written law or rule of law` IS `under written law`
        `lawfully done under a power for preventing or detecting crime` IS `crime prevention`
        `done on behalf of the Government and necessary for national security, defence or international relations` IS `national security`
        `a ministerial certificate under section 7(8) has been issued` IS `certificate`

-- Illustration to s 7(3)(b): Y repeatedly sends emails to the subordinate X with
-- suggestive comments about X's body.
`repeated suggestive emails to a subordinate` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 20 FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration to s 7(10): Y plants a camera in X's apartment and watches the
-- live feed continually over several days. "Y's conduct is protracted." One
-- occasion.
`the hidden camera, watched for days` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 1 TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- One occasion, not protracted, no previous conviction. Not a course of conduct.
`one unpleasant visit` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 1 FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- The same single occasion, by someone already convicted of stalking the same
-- victim. s 7(10)(a)(ii) makes it a course of conduct.
`one unpleasant visit by a convicted stalker` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 1 FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Illustration (g) to s 7(3): Y follows X's public page and checks for updates,
-- where X has taken no steps to stop it. "This act is NOT an act associated with
-- stalking."
`Illustration 7(g) -- following a public page` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 50 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- A reporter's persistent doorstepping, caused distress, and the course of
-- conduct was reasonable in all the circumstances.
`persistent conduct that was reasonable` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 10 FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

-- Surveillance by an agency, with a certificate under s 7(8). The certificate is
-- conclusive, so the limb is established even where the underlying fact is
-- recorded as false.
`surveillance with a ministerial certificate` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 30 TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE

-- Conduct repeated and distressing, where the accused neither intended the harm
-- nor would a reasonable person have thought it likely.
`repeated attention nobody would think distressing` MEANS
    `stalking facts` 10 FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

#ASSERT `there is a course of conduct within section 7(10)` `repeated suggestive emails to a subordinate`
#ASSERT `the accused unlawfully stalks the victim` `repeated suggestive emails to a subordinate`
#ASSERT `an offence under section 7(6) is committed` `repeated suggestive emails to a subordinate`

-- s 7(10)(a)(i): one protracted occasion is a course of conduct.
#ASSERT `there is a course of conduct within section 7(10)` `the hidden camera, watched for days`
#ASSERT `the accused unlawfully stalks the victim` `the hidden camera, watched for days`

-- s 7(10)(b): one occasion otherwise is not.
#ASSERT NOT `there is a course of conduct within section 7(10)` `one unpleasant visit`
#ASSERT NOT `the accused unlawfully stalks the victim` `one unpleasant visit`

-- s 7(10)(a)(ii): the same single occasion becomes a course of conduct because
-- of the accused's record. Identical conduct, different answer.
#ASSERT `there is a course of conduct within section 7(10)` `one unpleasant visit by a convicted stalker`
#ASSERT `the accused unlawfully stalks the victim` `one unpleasant visit by a convicted stalker`

-- Illustration (g): fifty occasions of conduct that is not associated with
-- stalking are not unlawful stalking.
#ASSERT `there is a course of conduct within section 7(10)` `Illustration 7(g) -- following a public page`
#ASSERT NOT `the accused unlawfully stalks the victim` `Illustration 7(g) -- following a public page`

-- s 7(2)(c): intent or the objective limb in s 7(4). Neither, and there is no
-- unlawful stalking however often it was repeated.
#ASSERT `the fault element in section 7(2)(c) is satisfied` `repeated suggestive emails to a subordinate`
#ASSERT NOT `the fault element in section 7(2)(c) is satisfied` `repeated attention nobody would think distressing`
#ASSERT NOT `the accused unlawfully stalks the victim` `repeated attention nobody would think distressing`

-- s 7(7), and the opening words of s 7(2). Here the defence negates the
-- contravention, not merely the offence.
#ASSERT `a defence in section 7(7) is made out` `persistent conduct that was reasonable`
#ASSERT NOT `the accused unlawfully stalks the victim` `persistent conduct that was reasonable`
#ASSERT NOT `section 7(1) is contravened` `persistent conduct that was reasonable`

-- s 7(8): the certificate is conclusive evidence.
#ASSERT NOT `surveillance with a ministerial certificate`'s `done on behalf of the Government and necessary for national security, defence or international relations`
#ASSERT `the national security limb of section 7(7)(d) is established` `surveillance with a ministerial certificate`
#ASSERT NOT `section 7(1) is contravened` `surveillance with a ministerial certificate`

§ `Sections 8, 8A, 8B and 8C -- the penalty arithmetic`

GIVEN `subsequent` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `vulnerable` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `could protect themselves` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `intimate` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `relationship did not matter` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A SentencingFacts
DECIDE `sentencing` `subsequent` `vulnerable` `could protect themselves` `intimate` `relationship did not matter` IS
    SentencingFacts WITH
        `a subsequent conviction within section 8` IS `subsequent`
        `the victim is a vulnerable person and the offender knew or ought to have known` IS `vulnerable`
        `the victim was capable of protecting himself or herself as an ordinary person would` IS `could protect themselves`
        `the victim was or is in an intimate relationship with the offender` IS `intimate`
        `the relationship did not adversely affect the victim's ability to protect themselves` IS `relationship did not matter`

`a first offence, nothing aggravating` MEANS `sentencing` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`a subsequent conviction` MEANS `sentencing` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`a first offence against a vulnerable person` MEANS `sentencing` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
`a first offence against a vulnerable person who could protect themselves` MEANS `sentencing` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
`a first offence against an intimate partner` MEANS `sentencing` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`a first offence against a vulnerable intimate partner` MEANS `sentencing` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`a subsequent conviction against a vulnerable intimate partner` MEANS `sentencing` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE

-- The baseline. Every Part 2 offence carries a $5,000 maximum fine on a first
-- conviction, and s 4 alone carries no imprisonment.
#ASSERT (`the penalty on a first conviction` `section 3`)'s `the maximum fine in dollars` EQUALS 5000
#ASSERT (`the penalty on a first conviction` `section 4`)'s `the maximum fine in dollars` EQUALS 5000
#ASSERT (`the penalty on a first conviction` `section 4`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`the penalty on a first conviction` `section 3`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 6
#ASSERT (`the penalty on a first conviction` `section 7`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 12

-- s 8: on a subsequent conviction, s 4 acquires an imprisonment term it did not
-- have. The repeat offender under s 4 can be imprisoned; the first offender
-- cannot, whatever the aggravation.
#ASSERT (`the penalty under section 8 on a subsequent conviction` `section 4`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 6
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 4` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 0
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 4` `a subsequent conviction`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 6

-- AND HERE IS THE ARITHMETIC. For ss 3, 5 and 7, s 8's fixed subsequent-
-- conviction maxima are exactly twice the first-conviction maxima -- which is
-- also what s 8A and s 8B produce. So for those three sections the two routes
-- give the identical ceiling, and the exclusion in s 8A(1) and s 8B(1) makes no
-- difference to it.
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum fine in dollars` EQUALS (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a subsequent conviction`)'s `the maximum fine in dollars`
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a subsequent conviction`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months`
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 5` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS (`the maximum punishment` `section 5` `a subsequent conviction`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months`
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 7` `a first offence against an intimate partner`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS (`the maximum punishment` `section 7` `a subsequent conviction`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months`

-- The figures themselves.
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum fine in dollars` EQUALS 10000
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 12
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 7` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 24
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence, nothing aggravating`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 6

-- s 8C: both enhancements applicable, one doubling.
#ASSERT `section 8A applies` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable intimate partner`
#ASSERT `section 8B applies` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable intimate partner`
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable intimate partner`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 12
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable intimate partner`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months`

-- s 8A(1) and s 8B(1) exclude themselves where s 8 applies, so the repeat
-- offender against a vulnerable intimate partner is in exactly the same position
-- as the first offender against a vulnerable person.
#ASSERT NOT `section 8A applies` `section 3` `a subsequent conviction against a vulnerable intimate partner`
#ASSERT NOT `section 8B applies` `section 3` `a subsequent conviction against a vulnerable intimate partner`
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a subsequent conviction against a vulnerable intimate partner`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 12

-- s 8A(2A): the escape for the offender who proves the victim could protect
-- themselves after all.
#ASSERT NOT `section 8A applies` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable person who could protect themselves`
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 3` `a first offence against a vulnerable person who could protect themselves`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 6

-- s 6 IS NOT IN s 8A OR s 8B. A public servant who is also a vulnerable person
-- gets no enhancement at all on a first conviction, where a victim of the same
-- conduct under s 4 would.
#ASSERT NOT `sections 8A and 8B reach this offence` `section 6`
#ASSERT `sections 8A and 8B reach this offence` `section 4`
#ASSERT NOT `section 8A applies` `section 6` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 6` `a first offence against a vulnerable person`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 12
#ASSERT (`the maximum punishment` `section 6` `a first offence, nothing aggravating`)'s `the maximum imprisonment in months` EQUALS 12

§ `Section 10 -- failing to comply with an order`

GIVEN `failed` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `it was the counselling referral` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved no knowledge` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `order in effect` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `punished as contempt` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NonComplianceFacts
DECIDE `non-compliance` `failed` `it was the counselling referral` `reasonable excuse` `proved no knowledge` `order in effect` `punished as contempt` IS
    NonComplianceFacts WITH
        `failed to comply with an order under section 12(2), 12(2E), 13(1) or 13(1B)` IS `failed`
        `what was not complied with is the counselling or mediation referral under section 12(2B)(c)` IS `it was the counselling referral`
        `had a reasonable excuse` IS `reasonable excuse`
        `proved absence of knowledge of the terms of the order` IS `proved no knowledge`
        `the order had taken effect` IS `order in effect`
        `the non-compliance has been punished as a contempt of court` IS `punished as contempt`

`plain breach of a protection order` MEANS `non-compliance` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`did not attend the counselling the order required` MEANS `non-compliance` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`breach with a reasonable excuse` MEANS `non-compliance` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
`breach by someone who proved they never knew the terms` MEANS `non-compliance` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
`breach already punished as a contempt` MEANS `non-compliance` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE

#ASSERT `an offence under section 10(1) is committed` `plain breach of a protection order`

-- The one part of a protection order that s 10 does not back: the s 12(2B)(c)
-- referral to counselling or mediation.
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 10(1) is committed` `did not attend the counselling the order required`

#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 10(1) is committed` `breach with a reasonable excuse`

-- s 10(1A): knowledge is presumed until the contrary is proved.
#ASSERT `the accused is taken to know the terms of the order` `plain breach of a protection order`
#ASSERT NOT `the accused is taken to know the terms of the order` `breach by someone who proved they never knew the terms`
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 10(1) is committed` `breach by someone who proved they never knew the terms`

-- s 10(4): election, not accumulation.
#ASSERT NOT `an offence under section 10(1) is committed` `breach already punished as a contempt`
